{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "the social network is a masterful exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the birth of a digital empire. directed by david fincher with a sharp screenplay by aaron sorkin, the film dissects the founding of facebook through a non-linear narrative that weaves together depositions and flashbacks. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain, but as a complex, socially awkward genius driven by a deep need for validation and connection. the supporting cast, including andrew garfield as the conflicted eduardo saverin and josh pehce as the charismatic sean parker, adds layers of emotional depth to the story of friendship turned into litigation. the dialogue is rapid-fire and witty, demanding full attention from the viewer, while trent reznor and atticus ross's haunting electronic score perfectly captures the cold, calculating atmosphere of the coding world. visually, the film is sleek and precise, utilizing cool color palettes and intimate close-ups to emphasize the isolation behind the screens. it is more than just a biopic; it is a tragic commentary on the cost of success in the modern age, raising questions about ownership, loyalty, and the price of changing the world. the film remains a gripping, thought-provoking piece of cinema that feels even more relevant years after its release.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.010175504}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0006070787}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.009055604}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.022259885}, {"token": "ful", "logprob": -0.6116368}, {"token": " exploration", "logprob": -0.14391904}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ambition", "logprob": -0.0004238192}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " betra